1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,635
How does water in a reservoir

2
00:00:02,675 --> 00:00:04,466
become electricity for a city?

3
00:00:06,702 --> 00:00:09,262
The journey combines hydraulic energy,

4
00:00:09,302 --> 00:00:12,223
mechanical motion and
electrical phenomena.

5
00:00:13,887 --> 00:00:15,592
We will follow it step by step

6
00:00:15,632 --> 00:00:17,681
and then see how the plant responds

7
00:00:17,721 --> 00:00:19,884
when the needs of the system change.

8
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:25,599
Head and flow determine

9
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:27,109
the energy we can use.

10
00:00:29,707 --> 00:00:32,269
Part of it is lost along the conduit;

11
00:00:32,309 --> 00:00:34,183
that is why we use the net head.

12
00:00:35,875 --> 00:00:39,095
In this example, a head of 400 metres

13
00:00:39,135 --> 00:00:42,029
and 8.5 cubic metres per second

14
00:00:42,069 --> 00:00:44,102
yield about 30 megawatts,

15
00:00:44,142 --> 00:00:46,783
after turbine and generator losses.

16
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,130
At the nozzle, the water pressure

17
00:00:55,170 --> 00:00:56,884
becomes a high-speed jet.

18
00:00:59,434 --> 00:01:01,127
The jet strikes the buckets

19
00:01:01,167 --> 00:01:03,346
of the Pelton turbine tangentially

20
00:01:03,386 --> 00:01:04,827
and changes direction.

21
00:01:06,026 --> 00:01:08,215
That interaction makes the runner turn.

22
00:01:12,272 --> 00:01:15,438
The shaft carries mechanical
power to the generator,

23
00:01:15,478 --> 00:01:17,654
while the water leaves
through the tailrace.

24
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:25,287
The rotor turns with the turbine.

25
00:01:26,149 --> 00:01:28,810
Its electromagnets
produce a magnetic field

26
00:01:28,850 --> 00:01:30,673
that moves past the windings

27
00:01:30,713 --> 00:01:33,365
of the stator, which stays fixed.

28
00:01:35,969 --> 00:01:37,914
That change induces voltage.

29
00:01:38,822 --> 00:01:40,180
The windings are arranged

30
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:41,638
to produce three phases.

31
00:01:44,014 --> 00:01:46,581
In this illustrative machine, 20 poles

32
00:01:46,621 --> 00:01:49,743
and 360 revolutions per minute

33
00:01:49,783 --> 00:01:51,590
correspond to 60 hertz.

34
00:01:55,200 --> 00:01:57,974
Before connecting the generator,

35
00:01:58,014 --> 00:01:59,929
its voltage, frequency and phase angle

36
00:01:59,969 --> 00:02:01,615
must match those of the grid.

37
00:02:04,310 --> 00:02:05,862
Once the breaker closes,

38
00:02:05,902 --> 00:02:07,080
it can deliver power.

39
00:02:09,637 --> 00:02:11,844
The transformer steps the voltage up

40
00:02:11,884 --> 00:02:15,565
from 13.8 to 230 kilovolts.

41
00:02:16,233 --> 00:02:18,300
The same power is then transmitted

42
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:20,478
with less current and lower losses

43
00:02:20,518 --> 00:02:21,685
in the conductors.

44
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:28,076
Energy travels along the transmission line

45
00:02:28,116 --> 00:02:31,513
and reaches substations
that step the voltage down.

46
00:02:32,155 --> 00:02:34,663
It then passes through subtransmission

47
00:02:34,703 --> 00:02:36,562
and medium-voltage feeders.

48
00:02:37,957 --> 00:02:41,078
A distribution transformer
adapts the supply

49
00:02:41,118 --> 00:02:42,758
for low-voltage users.

50
00:02:44,943 --> 00:02:46,849
Every level needs switching,

51
00:02:46,889 --> 00:02:48,949
metering and protection equipment

52
00:02:48,989 --> 00:02:51,730
to operate safely and
without interruption.

53
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:58,829
To observe the response,

54
00:02:58,869 --> 00:03:00,860
we isolate an equivalent system

55
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:02,213
and raise its demand

56
00:03:02,253 --> 00:03:04,704
from 20 to 23 megawatts.

57
00:03:06,512 --> 00:03:08,060
At first, mechanical power

58
00:03:08,100 --> 00:03:09,816
does not change instantly:

59
00:03:11,927 --> 00:03:14,442
rotating energy helps cover the deficit

60
00:03:14,482 --> 00:03:16,125
and the frequency falls.

61
00:03:18,624 --> 00:03:20,471
The governor admits more water

62
00:03:20,511 --> 00:03:22,113
and delivers more power.

63
00:03:24,492 --> 00:03:27,073
Primary regulation arrests the drop;

64
00:03:28,315 --> 00:03:30,926
a secondary action adjusts the setpoint

65
00:03:30,966 --> 00:03:33,999
to bring the frequency back to 60 hertz.

66
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:41,126
A rise in reactive demand

67
00:03:41,166 --> 00:03:44,065
can lower the voltage at
the connection point.

68
00:03:45,889 --> 00:03:48,116
Here another control steps in:

69
00:03:48,156 --> 00:03:50,145
the automatic voltage regulator.

70
00:03:51,545 --> 00:03:53,262
It adjusts the rotor excitation

71
00:03:53,302 --> 00:03:56,186
to change the electrical
response of the generator

72
00:03:56,226 --> 00:03:57,705
and support the voltage.

73
00:03:59,428 --> 00:04:00,713
Its action is limited

74
00:04:00,753 --> 00:04:02,929
by the capability of the machine.

75
00:04:04,048 --> 00:04:06,202
The governor and the excitation system

76
00:04:06,242 --> 00:04:07,900
perform different functions,

77
00:04:07,940 --> 00:04:10,208
although they work within the same system.

78
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:19,084
Water supplies energy, the
turbine produces motion

79
00:04:19,124 --> 00:04:22,194
and the generator converts
it into electricity.

80
00:04:23,108 --> 00:04:24,803
Transformers make it possible

81
00:04:24,843 --> 00:04:26,818
to transmit and distribute it.

82
00:04:27,659 --> 00:04:29,217
Controls and protection

83
00:04:29,257 --> 00:04:31,334
keep operation within limits.

84
00:04:32,995 --> 00:04:36,241
Understanding the plant means
following that whole chain,

85
00:04:36,281 --> 00:04:37,504
from the water resource

86
00:04:37,544 --> 00:04:38,957
to the needs of the user.
